{:edb/db-before-t 2001 :edb/db-after-t 2002 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535314 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:52:56.000Z" 13194139535314 true] [17592186179641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343274 13194139535314 true] [17592186179646 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343267 13194139535314 true] [17592186179656 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343281 13194139535314 true] [17592186179915 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535314 true] [17592186179915 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061629/assets/0b0f3ae9a464812957c297c09ff3f65bfe5964bdac70298e72ec3dbde2325e72.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $6{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6,$ shown above?" 13194139535314 true] [17592186179915 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3690/Source/Images/q-123193.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral $∫_(C)(6x^{2} - y^{2})ds$, where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $6x^{2} + y^{2} = 6$, shown above?" 13194139535314 false] [17592186179915 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $\\sqrt{6},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =6{cos}^{2}⁡t-6{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =6({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 true] [17592186180117 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535314 true] [17592186180117 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061629/assets/78f8ca3e70afb8377748a18d7024ba297e330add59550a41a87025a0145b56bb.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}9xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4$  traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" 13194139535314 true] [17592186180117 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3690/Source/Images/q-41145.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral $∫_(C)9xyds$, where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse $x^{2} + 4y^{2} = 4$ traversed from the point $(2, 0)$ to the point $(0, 1)$." 13194139535314 false] [17592186180117 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(8-1) \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343267 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343268 13194139535314 true] [17592186343267 1089 "q-123143" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343267 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535314 true] [17592186343267 1100 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+49{y}^{2}=196,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343267 1113 #uuid "7fb44864-edbc-5a3d-bf6d-c1269d74e67b" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343267 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{7}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $7$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=7\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }7\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343269 13194139535314 true] [17592186343268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343270 13194139535314 true] [17592186343268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343271 13194139535314 true] [17592186343268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343272 13194139535314 true] [17592186343268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343273 13194139535314 true] [17592186343268 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535314 true] [17592186343268 1041 "selection" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343268 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343272 13194139535314 true] [17592186343268 1110 #uuid "6cfe3a2a-5cb5-515e-bc99-cc3e0abc75be" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343269 1034 "7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+45}\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343269 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535314 true] [17592186343269 1111 #uuid "92a0d836-c5eb-5cb5-9a9c-f4c6843eeff0" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343270 1034 "7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343270 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535314 true] [17592186343270 1111 #uuid "935feaba-b58b-536d-a925-4d51a9c3eb6d" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343271 1034 "14{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343271 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535314 true] [17592186343271 1111 #uuid "61aca6c6-ea12-5134-8a15-4892a48b3d89" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343272 1034 "7{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{45{sin}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535314 true] [17592186343272 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535314 t